WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:03.702
Good morning, listeners.

2
00:00:03.652 --> 00:00:07.439
Today is October 25, 2026.

3
00:00:07.439 --> 00:00:15.344
Overnight, Wall Street desks assessed a relentless high-rate regime pressuring the S and P five hundred, Nasdaq, and Dow.

4
00:00:15.344 --> 00:00:22.525
The Federal Reserve remains entrenched in its tightening cycle, anchoring global valuation multiples under heavy gravity.

5
00:00:22.525 --> 00:00:29.792
Ten-year Treasury yields held near five point two percent after touching five point three five percent on October eighth.

6
00:00:29.792 --> 00:00:37.317
That multi-year yield peak is actively compressing equity risk premiums across the S-P-Y and Q-Q-Q benchmarks.

7
00:00:37.317 --> 00:00:44.118
Market bulls argue resilient corporate cash flows can absorb higher borrowing costs across core indices.

8
00:00:44.118 --> 00:00:52.709
Bears counter that risk-free rates above five percent mathematically break defensive bond-proxy valuations and strangle lower-income consumers.

9
00:00:52.709 --> 00:01:00.393
Across the United States, sticky inflation and elevated oil prices are visibly eroding working-class purchasing power.

10
00:01:00.393 --> 00:01:06.741
Meanwhile, Europe is undergoing a forced structural dismantling of its half-century post-war comfort order.

11
00:01:06.741 --> 00:01:13.738
Violent unrest has spread across France, Portugal, and Italy alongside crippling European port strikes.

12
00:01:13.738 --> 00:01:20.246
Mediterranean shipping lanes face paralysis while winter natural gas prices spike across the continent.

13
00:01:20.246 --> 00:01:30.625
This instability reflects four colliding forces: technological disruption, geopolitical realignment, industrial hyper-competition, and demographic reversal.

14
00:01:30.625 --> 00:01:37.084
After abandoning single-source Russian gas, Europe has not yet built a resilient clean energy grid.

15
00:01:37.084 --> 00:01:43.542
President Trump restricted his promised five-thousand-dollar stimulus checks strictly to domestic American spending.

16
00:01:43.542 --> 00:01:50.515
He explicitly barred those funds from flowing into turbulent France, while Iran protested French police force.

17
00:01:50.515 --> 00:01:57.795
Inside France, rigid welfare liabilities and extreme public resource misallocation have reached a breaking point.

18
00:01:57.795 --> 00:02:04.951
France employs five point nine million public servants, yet vast personnel sit in back-office administration.

19
00:02:04.951 --> 00:02:11.508
Frontline classrooms face chronic teacher shortages while pensions and debt interest consume the national budget.

20
00:02:11.508 --> 00:02:19.241
Remaining education funds prioritize retired staff, leaving over half of French public schools older than fifty years.

21
00:02:19.241 --> 00:02:25.564
Welfare politics obey an irreversible ratchet where benefits can only expand and never contract.

22
00:02:25.564 --> 00:02:29.547
Demographics compound this fiscal trap across the continent.

23
00:02:29.547 --> 00:02:36.324
Maintaining stable population structures requires a replacement fertility rate of two point one children per woman.

24
00:02:36.324 --> 00:02:42.500
Germany sits at one point three six, while Spain and Italy have fallen below one point two.

25
00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:48.040
Even France, Europe's demographic ceiling, registers just one point six one.

26
00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:54.241
Decades ago, Europe imported cheap labor to preserve short working hours and generous welfare.

27
00:02:54.241 --> 00:02:59.927
First-generation immigrants worked dutifully and delivered a ten-to-twenty-year tax dividend.

28
00:02:59.927 --> 00:03:04.584
Now that first generation is aging into pension and healthcare claims.

29
00:03:04.584 --> 00:03:13.407
Simultaneously, second and third generations remain isolated in under-resourced suburbs facing cultural fracture and blocked mobility.

30
00:03:13.407 --> 00:03:19.608
Europe harvested short-term labor dividends by mortgaging thirty to fifty years of social cohesion.

31
00:03:19.608 --> 00:03:26.496
Beneath these pressures lies the Eurozone's congenital flaw: unified money paired with fractured fiscal authority.

32
00:03:26.496 --> 00:03:33.812
Created after German reunification to bind Berlin into a shared system, the Eurozone lacks a unified treasury.

33
00:03:33.812 --> 00:03:40.380
Member states routinely breach the three percent deficit cap and sixty percent debt ceiling without penalty.

34
00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:47.390
Rich nations refuse to backstop poorer neighbors, while indebted states resist European Central Bank tightening.

35
00:03:47.504 --> 00:03:54.586
This European structural crisis is driving a massive reallocation of global tech and industrial capital flows.

36
00:03:54.536 --> 00:04:04.095
Europe still holds three crown jewels: global regulatory standard-setting power, hidden-champion precision manufacturers, and elite basic science.

37
00:04:04.095 --> 00:04:10.516
Yet in commercial technology, Europe has devolved from a global rule-maker into a distressed asset seller.

38
00:04:10.516 --> 00:04:14.217
The solar industry offers a stark historical warning.

39
00:04:14.217 --> 00:04:20.320
Germany pioneered feed-in tariffs and grid standards, creating the initial global solar market.

40
00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:27.563
However, high European energy and labor costs could not survive asset-heavy manufacturing scale competition.

41
00:04:27.563 --> 00:04:35.798
Local factories collapsed, leaving Europe a passive importer now facing waves of retiring solar panels without recycling capacity.

42
00:04:35.798 --> 00:04:42.979
Over the past twenty years, Brussels avoided foundational tech venture risk in favor of regulatory monetization.

43
00:04:42.979 --> 00:04:50.247
Through the G-D-P-R, the Digital Markets Act, and the A-I Act, Europe levied heavy compliance fines.

44
00:04:50.247 --> 00:04:57.979
American tech titans easily absorbed those legal costs, but compliance barriers strangled European startups in the cradle.

45
00:04:57.979 --> 00:05:06.484
This regulatory boomerang caused Europe to miss the mobile internet, cloud computing, big data, and artificial intelligence waves.

46
00:05:06.484 --> 00:05:11.962
Traditional German manufacturing has simultaneously lost its four historic pillars.

47
00:05:11.962 --> 00:05:19.254
Cheap Russian gas is gone, internal combustion moats are eroding, and domestic battery expansion has collapsed.

48
00:05:19.254 --> 00:05:25.492
European automakers were ultimately forced to walk back their aggressive internal combustion engine ban.

49
00:05:25.492 --> 00:05:31.815
At the Berlin Summit, Nvidia chief Jensen Huang confronted European leaders with a piercing question.

50
00:05:31.815 --> 00:05:37.269
He asked what Europe is actually regulating if its domestic industry never gets built at all.

51
00:05:37.269 --> 00:05:43.629
That summit exposed a four-layered industrial capitulation between Germany and American tech leadership.

52
00:05:43.629 --> 00:05:51.117
Europe openly admitted it lacks independent compute, algorithms, large language models, and engineering scale.

53
00:05:51.117 --> 00:05:58.899
To survive the industrial A-I transition, Germany is surrendering a century of proprietary manufacturing data sovereignty.

54
00:05:58.899 --> 00:06:07.281
Hidden-champion process parameters, equipment logic, and precision production data are being packaged directly for Nvidia model training.

55
00:06:07.281 --> 00:06:14.046
On top of surrendering data, European industry is paying tens of billions of euros to buy Nvidia compute.

56
00:06:14.046 --> 00:06:22.011
For semiconductor investors tracking the S-O-X-X benchmark, Europe's industrial bailout is a massive capital inflow catalyst.

57
00:06:22.011 --> 00:06:30.933
Turning to our individual equity deep dives, Nvidia, ticker N-V-D-A, stands as the primary beneficiary of Europe's industrial pivot.

58
00:06:30.933 --> 00:06:38.457
By absorbing a century of German mechanical engineering data, Nvidia is building an unassailable physical A-I moat.

59
00:06:38.457 --> 00:06:45.418
Sovereign and industrial European orders worth tens of billions of euros lock in multi-year data center visibility.

60
00:06:45.418 --> 00:06:54.180
While high Treasury yields cap broad index multiples, Nvidia captures mandatory survival Capex from legacy global manufacturers.

61
00:06:54.320 --> 00:07:03.007
In the consumer sector, Starbucks, ticker S-B-U-X, provides the definitive blueprint for operational turnarounds under inflation pressure.

62
00:07:02.957 --> 00:07:08.729
Over a year ago, Starbucks saw its American transaction volume drop four percent.

63
00:07:08.729 --> 00:07:15.837
Rather than relying on margin-destroying price cuts, leadership executed its Back to Starbucks operational overhaul.

64
00:07:15.837 --> 00:07:23.031
By restoring store experience and service speed, American transactions turned positive in late 2025.

65
00:07:23.031 --> 00:07:30.543
Starbucks delivered over four percent transaction growth across the past two quarters, hitting four point two percent recently.

66
00:07:30.543 --> 00:07:37.847
Shares rallied from seventy-nine dollars in October 2025 to one hundred six dollars in August 2026.

67
00:07:37.847 --> 00:07:45.286
Select fast-casual peers are also proving that strong value propositions can win market share during a traffic recession.

68
00:07:45.286 --> 00:07:53.815
Blackbox data shows overall American fast-food traffic was negative in 2019 and from 2022 through 2025.

69
00:07:53.815 --> 00:08:01.744
Sector traffic fell one point four percent in first-quarter 2026 and dropped up to two point four percent monthly through August.

70
00:08:01.744 --> 00:08:10.445
Yet Cava grew traffic five point three percent, Texas Roadhouse gained three percent, and Dutch Bros rose one point seven percent.

71
00:08:10.445 --> 00:08:14.489
Chipotle also turned traffic positive at one percent.

72
00:08:14.489 --> 00:08:22.528
Conversely, Wendy's hiked average ticket prices by five point six percent and suffered a twelve point five percent traffic plunge.

73
00:08:22.528 --> 00:08:28.852
Wendy's comparable sales dropped seven percent, proving that pure price gouging destroys volume.

74
00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:41.278
That brings us to our centerpiece deep dive: McDonald's, ticker M-C-D. Shares have tumbled roughly thirty percent over the past half year, sliding from late-February highs near three hundred thirty-five dollars.

75
00:08:41.228 --> 00:08:47.919
The stock now trades around two hundred thirty-five dollars, repeatedly printing new fifty-two-week lows.

76
00:08:47.919 --> 00:08:54.598
Forward 2027 price-to-earnings valuation has compressed to a five-year low of seventeen times.

77
00:08:54.598 --> 00:09:00.922
Two forces are crushing the stock: macro yield compression and a broken domestic traffic engine.

78
00:09:00.922 --> 00:09:09.451
With ten-year Treasury yields above five point two percent, McDonald's three point three percent dividend yield has lost its defensive appeal.

79
00:09:09.451 --> 00:09:15.260
Operationally, international markets generate sixty percent of revenue and remain steady.

80
00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:24.292
Second-quarter 2026 international operated comparable sales rose one point five percent, while licensed markets grew one point nine percent.

81
00:09:24.292 --> 00:09:30.480
JPMorgan projects third-quarter international comparable growth will hold between two and three percent.

82
00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:37.000
However, the American business, representing forty percent of revenue, has become the primary drag.

83
00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:45.885
Since 2025, American comparable sales have swung wildly between minus three point six percent and plus six point eight percent.

84
00:09:45.885 --> 00:09:52.674
Second-quarter 2026 domestic comps edged up just zero point eight percent on negative foot traffic.

85
00:09:52.674 --> 00:09:58.716
JPMorgan expects third-quarter American comparable sales to contract by roughly one percent.

86
00:09:58.716 --> 00:10:04.451
The root cause is severe price-volume distortion after years of aggressive menu inflation.

87
00:10:04.451 --> 00:10:10.738
Mass consumer staples compound wealth through recurring traffic volume rather than one-off price hikes.

88
00:10:10.738 --> 00:10:17.895
Yet McDonald's raised American menu prices by ten percent annually in both 2022 and 2023.

89
00:10:17.895 --> 00:10:24.549
By 2024, average menu prices sat roughly forty percent above 2019 levels.

90
00:10:24.549 --> 00:10:29.917
Domestic foot traffic officially turned negative in the third quarter of 2023.

91
00:10:29.917 --> 00:10:38.029
Comparable sales turned negative in second-quarter 2024 and hit minus three point six percent in first-quarter 2025.

92
00:10:38.029 --> 00:10:46.608
Across the last ten quarters, American traffic was positive only twice, driven entirely by five-dollar meals and heavy promotions.

93
00:10:46.608 --> 00:10:53.985
To restore everyday value, headquarters launched a sub-three-dollar value menu alongside a store modernization push.

94
00:10:53.985 --> 00:11:01.105
Upgrading a traditional American Drive-Thru with new kitchens and A-I systems costs one million dollars per store.

95
00:11:01.105 --> 00:11:07.306
Because ninety-five percent of McDonald's restaurants are franchised, this sparked an internal revolt.

96
00:11:07.306 --> 00:11:17.147
In January 2026, headquarters tightened national value pricing standards, forcing franchisees to absorb discounting and food inflation costs.

97
00:11:17.147 --> 00:11:24.525
A National Owners Association survey revealed ninety-five percent of franchisees suffered first-quarter profit declines.

98
00:11:24.525 --> 00:11:29.463
Nearly eighty percent lack the cash flow required for store reinvestments.

99
00:11:29.463 --> 00:11:38.409
Kalinowski franchisee relationship scores fell from one point seven one last October to one point three seven in January 2026.

100
00:11:38.409 --> 00:11:45.199
Consequently, only sixty to sixty-five percent of stores currently execute the sub-three-dollar value menu.

101
00:11:45.199 --> 00:11:53.422
On September twenty-third, management unveiled an eight-point-five-billion-dollar franchisee support package running through 2036.

102
00:11:53.422 --> 00:12:00.836
Five billion dollars will be deployed before 2030, including up to two billion in direct remodel co-investments.

103
00:12:00.836 --> 00:12:06.657
Another three to three point five billion dollars will fund rent relief and remodel subsidies.

104
00:12:06.657 --> 00:12:15.370
With subsidies, franchisee remodel payback drops to four years, generating one hundred thousand dollars in new annual store cash flow.

105
00:12:15.370 --> 00:12:21.350
Management targets lifting operating margins to the low-to-mid fifty percent range by 2030.

106
00:12:21.350 --> 00:12:29.782
However, shares dropped five percent on Investor Day because high-margin franchise rent relief hits corporate profits starting in 2027.

107
00:12:29.782 --> 00:12:37.417
Corporate payback will take five to six years, capping five-year consensus earnings growth at five to eight percent annually.

108
00:12:37.417 --> 00:12:44.255
JPMorgan slashed its 2027 earnings estimate by nine percent to just one point eight percent growth.

109
00:12:44.255 --> 00:12:50.762
The bank also cut its McDonald's price target from two hundred eighty dollars down to two hundred sixty dollars.

110
00:12:50.762 --> 00:12:54.328
History offers two turnaround benchmarks for

111
00:12:54.440 --> 00:12:56.154
McDonald's.

112
00:12:56.104 --> 00:13:04.965
During the 2002 crisis, shares fell forty percent before management cut store openings from twelve hundred forty-seven to five hundred thirteen.

113
00:13:04.965 --> 00:13:11.925
They deployed nine hundred field staff, launched the one-dollar menu, and rolled out the I'm Lovin' It campaign.

114
00:13:11.925 --> 00:13:19.683
American comps turned positive in four months, and the stock surged seventy-two percent from March to December 2003.

115
00:13:19.683 --> 00:13:28.040
In 2015, after food safety scandals and a one point four eight franchisee score, a new chief executive took over.

116
00:13:28.040 --> 00:13:37.146
Management refranchised four thousand stores, cut five hundred million dollars in overhead, and launched All-Day Breakfast in October 2015.

117
00:13:37.146 --> 00:13:45.038
Comps turned positive within seven months, sparking a fourteen percent single-month rally and multi-year double-digit earnings growth.

118
00:13:45.038 --> 00:13:53.531
Today's Next plan pushes franchise ownership to ninety-eight percent, but lacks a proven traffic hit while hand-breaded chicken remains in testing.

119
00:13:53.531 --> 00:14:01.545
At seventeen times forward earnings, further profit downgrades could make the valuation a trap around two hundred thirty-five dollars.

120
00:14:01.545 --> 00:14:06.852
Traders should avoid heavy bottom-fishing until American foot traffic turns positive.

121
00:14:06.852 --> 00:14:16.582
However, because fast food is completely decoupled from A-I disruption, long-term investors can accumulate small defensive tranches to hedge tech concentration.

122
00:14:16.582 --> 00:14:24.548
On the forward catalyst calendar, watch early November third-quarter earnings to see if McDonald's American comps breach minus one percent.

123
00:14:24.548 --> 00:14:33.616
In early 2027, monitor new entry-level value tiers, hand-breaded chicken launch dates, and initial rent subsidy margin impacts.

124
00:14:33.616 --> 00:14:43.616
Across global macro, track European winter natural gas prices, port strike settlements, and industrial A-I compute deployments following the Berlin Summit.

125
00:14:43.616 --> 00:14:47.624
That concludes today's XiaoFang Finance Morning Brief.

126
00:14:47.624 --> 00:14:51.790
Thank you for listening, trade safely, and see you tomorrow.
